00:05:46.225 --> 00:05:47.600
For today's
interview, of course,

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.630
we're diving more specifically
into quantum and post-quantum

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:51.320
security.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:52.778
Could you guys give
us a little bit

00:05:52.778 --> 00:05:55.220
of an understanding of what
is quantum computing before we

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.060
dive into the security
element of it?

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.290
ADAM LANGLEY: So computing as we
have known it for decades now,

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.320
since Turing's
time, has all been

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.500
based on classical
physics, more or less,

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:10.920
although down at the very
depths of how a CPU is made,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.807
there were quantum effects.

00:06:12.807 --> 00:06:15.140
What we're trying to build
is a computer that deals with

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.310
1's and 0's.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:19.220
And you could have
worked out, in theory,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.090
how these computers work
in the 1800s if you wanted.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:27.560
A quantum computer is a computer
that's obviously not just made

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.560
in the quantum
universe, but uses

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.160
effects of quantum physics.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:37.640
And it's difficult to convey
quite what that means.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.580
But there are certain
problems where

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.420
the ability, in quantum
physics, to have states

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:49.220
that are a mixture of 1's
and 0's and to use the fact

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:52.010
that, in quantum
physics, probabilities

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.350
are the square root
of sums of squares

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.600
rather than the
sums and things--

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.270
where those abilities
allow you to solve

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:04.370
some problems significantly
faster, and many problems quite

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.444
a lot faster.

00:07:05.444 --> 00:07:06.360
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.210
Nick, was there anything
you wanted to add?

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.370
NICK SULLIVAN: Yeah,
so quantum computing

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:12.770
is something that
involves, as Adam said,

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:15.140
quantum physics and
quantum interactions.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.320
And the more classical
way of thinking about this

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.740
is-- you know the idea of
Schrodinger's cat, where

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:22.720
you have a cat.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.160
It's inside an enclosed region.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.650
And it can be in a superposition
of is the cat alive,

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.220
or is the cat dead?

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:34.640
So because you can't see it
and you can't interact with it,

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.150
quantum effects allow
multiple states of being

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.620
to exist simultaneously.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.670
And the first time that you
actually interact with it

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:46.700
or look at it, it
immediately crystallizes

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:50.180
as one specific state,
whether the cat is alive

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:51.380
or the cat is dead.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:56.060
So this idea of interactions
between superpositions

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:59.330
of different states is
one of the key components

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:00.840
of quantum computing.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.970
And it's not something that you
can do classically with bits,

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:06.190
1's and 0's.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.600
As Adam mentioned,
classical computers

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:12.410
involve a whole series
of ons and offs.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:15.080
And then they can
interact together with one

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.600
on turns another one off,
turns another one on.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.670
And you can build
classical math with this.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:24.770
You can add things by
representing numbers in binary.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.510
With quantum computers,
or quantum computing,

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:30.090
you can do something
slightly more complex,

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:33.230
which is you can have
switches that are both on

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:37.280
and off simultaneously
and intertwined in a way

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:41.000
that once you finally look
at it or interact with it,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.970
it gets stabilized
and crystallized

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:43.897
in one specific state.

00:08:43.897 --> 00:08:45.980
MARK MANDEL: Adam, you
said something interesting.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.170
You said quantum computing
allows you to solve

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:49.880
certain problems faster.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:50.880
Why is that?

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:54.380
ADAM LANGLEY: So there's one,
let's say, set of problems

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.710
where quantum computing
essentially gives you

00:08:57.710 --> 00:08:59.840
a square root speed up.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.250
So the classical example
here is unstructured search.

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.860
If you have an unordered
list of numbers

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:10.400
and you want to find a number,
in the classical world,

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.280
the only thing you can do there
is look through the numbers one

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:14.270
by one.

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.280
And you expect to
find what you're

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:21.560
looking for, on average, in half
the number of numbers you have.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.830
Now, with a quantum computer,
somewhat mind-bendingly,

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:28.190
you can do it in only a square
root of the number of numbers

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:29.120
you have.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.140
And I have no good way to give
an intuitive explanation of why

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:38.690
that is so, but it involves the
way that probabilities evolve.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.820
Probabilities in the quantum
world are always squared.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:46.370
So in a classical world,
if you check one number,

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:49.100
you expect a 1 over n
chance of having found

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:50.680
the one you're looking for.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.300
And in the quantum
world, you get a 1

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.940
over square root of n
chance each time you look.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.030
And so in the quantum
world, once you've

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:01.640
looked at the square
root of n, then

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.265
you've got a fraction
that's approaching 1.

00:10:04.265 --> 00:10:06.960
So that does not--

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:10.220
and it is important that you
solve exponential problems

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:11.692
faster than exponentially.

00:10:11.692 --> 00:10:13.400
So the square root of
2 to the n is still

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:15.460
an exponential problem.

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:19.150
However, there is a
set of problems where--

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.140
they're not
exponential problems,

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:24.570
but we don't-- or
some of them are.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:27.690
We don't know any classical,
efficient way to solve them,

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.750
but we do know an
efficient way to solve them

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.130
on quantum computers.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.340
And the effect there is much
bigger than the square root.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.070
It is going from
a small exponent,

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.680
say, to just a polynomial.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.130
And so for these problems,
quantum computers

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:43.880
make a huge difference.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.610
And unfortunately,
that sort of problems

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.800
includes all the
problems on which

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.824
we base public key
cryptography on today.

00:10:50.824 --> 00:10:53.240
MELANIE WARRICK: So then that
gets to-- part of the reason

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:55.670
why we wanted to do this
discussion was because we know

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.520
quantum computing is becoming
more mainstream in terms

00:10:58.520 --> 00:10:59.604
of people are aware of it.

00:10:59.604 --> 00:11:01.186
And then they hear,
well, that's going

00:11:01.186 --> 00:11:03.110
to break all the codes
once we've achieved it.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:06.440
So the question on
many people's minds

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:08.540
is, what are the
security issues?

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.960
What's the reality
of quantum computing?

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.340
NICK SULLIVAN: Yeah, so
one thing that people think

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.190
about when you talk about
quantum computing is, wow,

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:17.930
you have all these
bits, and you can

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:21.710
do an infinite number of
things together at once

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:23.930
and compute these massively
difficult problems

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:25.580
that you can't normally do.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:27.020
This actually is not the case.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:30.050
Quantum computing is
useful for speeding up

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:33.020
a very small set of
problems, one of which

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.390
is searching through
an unordered list.

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:39.110
Other ones would be simulating
quantum interactions.

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:41.270
So if you're in
physics and you want

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:44.776
to understand how different
photons interact together,

00:11:44.776 --> 00:11:46.400
quantum computing is
an interesting way

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:47.690
to simulate that.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:52.250
And the other one is,
interestingly enough,

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:53.540
factoring numbers.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:55.250
So if you have a
number and you want

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:57.380
to know what the
prime factors are,

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:00.080
what quantum computers
allow you to do,

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.900
via an algorithm called
Shor's algorithm--

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:04.460
we don't have to
go into it, but it

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.950
allows you to take a big
number and find out what

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.030
its prime factors are quickly.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:13.790
It also is useful for related
problems around that field.

00:12:13.790 --> 00:12:17.390
But the interesting part
is that factoring numbers

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.880
and being able to
do what's called

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.580
a discrete logarithm, which is
almost equivalent to factoring

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:27.020
numbers, lets you break almost
all modern cryptography.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.780
You have a big enough quantum
computer that has enough bits

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:31.900
that you can
intertwine together,

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:35.720
you can actually take any
modern cryptographic algorithm

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.880
that we use and figure
out what the key is.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.840
So this is somewhat
scary, you could say,

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:45.020
to how we communicate online
and how we share information.

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:46.970
Cryptography is pervasive.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.530
If you're accessing
Gmail or you're

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.600
accessing any sort of
website on the internet

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:52.940
or sending chats
to your friends,

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.320
we use modern cryptography
all the time for this.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.780
So with a sufficiently
large quantum computer,

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:03.560
there's risks that this
cryptography can be undone.

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.230
MELANIE WARRICK: So should we
be panicking about security?

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:07.105
ADAM LANGLEY: Uh, no.

00:13:07.105 --> 00:13:08.000
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.390
MELANIE WARRICK: I'm pretty much
trying to lead to that answer.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:12.634
So don't panic.

00:13:12.634 --> 00:13:14.300
ADAM LANGLEY: There
may be a deep reason

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.660
why quantum computing
causes us so many problems.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:20.570
We've based our
cryptography on problems

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:22.160
that we don't have
enough structure

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:24.590
to solve efficiently
with classical computers,

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:28.760
but they have enough structure
to be quick and to be small.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:31.940
And so we've huddled up
against this boundary of what

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:33.890
we could previously
solve efficiently,

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:36.560
and now quantum computers
are moving that boundary

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:37.700
out a little bit.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.660
So there's two reasons in
which why we shouldn't panic.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:44.240
Firstly, discussions
like-- many groups

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:46.550
are now building quantum
computers, small quantum

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.280
computers, but quantum
computers nonetheless.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:51.260
They have quite
high error rates.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:53.300
It's very difficult to
build a quantum computer

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:54.920
because the rest of
the universe tends

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.710
to leak in and
cause decoherence,

00:13:57.710 --> 00:13:59.630
and messes up your
quantum states,

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:01.089
and everything goes wrong.

00:14:01.089 --> 00:14:02.880
And when that happens,
the quantum computer

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:04.820
will produce the wrong answer.

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:08.970
And so the error rates we have
at the moment are quite high.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:12.770
We believe that
given enough quantum

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:15.800
bits with a low
enough error rate,

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:17.870
we can run
error-correcting codes,

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.680
and so produce a theoretically
perfect quantum bit out

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:23.150
of real quantum bits.

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.310
But it takes a lot of them.

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.830
So it is not the
case that if you

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:31.260
see that some
group has a quantum

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:32.750
computer with some
dozens of bits,

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:36.110
then once that number reaches
2,048 or some other number

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.080
you may have heard in
relation to cryptography,

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.010
that everything is
gone and broken.

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:42.740
It depends on the technology,
but perhaps 100,000

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:47.380
to a million raw qubits to
make just one functional one.

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:49.130
MELANIE WARRICK: Is
that quantum supremacy

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:51.450
that you're referencing--

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:52.700
I've heard that term before--

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:53.991
in terms of the number of bits?

00:14:53.991 --> 00:14:55.990
NICK SULLIVAN:
Quantum supremacy is--

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:58.310
it's an artificial concept.

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:02.150
Quantum supremacy is the idea
that there is an algorithm out

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:07.160
there that a quantum computer
can demonstrably solve faster

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:11.400
than a classical computer, and
we haven't hit that point yet.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:16.160
And as I mentioned earlier, some
of the more interesting ideas

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:19.220
of things that you want to
do with quantum computers

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:22.940
involve simulating
physics situations.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:26.970
And so the target that people
have for quantum supremacy

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:30.146
is this algorithm
called sampling,

00:15:30.146 --> 00:15:32.270
where you take a lot of
bits, and you scramble them

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:35.150
up together, and they
supposedly represent

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:39.230
a certain random, but
structured-- random

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.250
association of probabilities.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:44.270
And once you actually look
at them, they decohere.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:47.300
And you say, OK, this fits
a specific distribution,

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:48.860
whether it's a
Gaussian distribution,

00:15:48.860 --> 00:15:50.780
or a croissant
distribution, or something

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:53.220
like that, some
statistical representation.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:57.650
So the idea is that
the first time that you

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:00.680
can get to a point where
a quantum computer can

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:04.250
simulate a random pattern
or a random distribution

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:05.870
faster than a
classical computer,

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:08.110
then it's somewhat supreme.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:10.170
There's some type of supremacy.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.560
So that's the idea
of when a quantum

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:16.010
computer can beat a classical
computer at one specific point.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:19.040
This is not really
related to cryptography.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:22.700
These problems that people
solve with quantum supremacy

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:24.650
or to demonstrate
quantum supremacy

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.230
are really niche
problems that aren't

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:30.870
really applicable to
breaking modern cryptography.

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:33.590
So even if this were
to happen next year

00:16:33.590 --> 00:16:36.080
or in the next five
years, it wouldn't

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:39.900
cause the collapse of our
financial system or everyone

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.180
to lose their privacy.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:44.930
It really takes a lot more
to find the point in which

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:46.760
cryptography can be broken.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:47.450
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's great to hear.

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:49.550
And Adam, I know you were
saying that the error

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.130
rate is really high so that's
why this is not as much

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:53.026
of a concern.

00:16:53.026 --> 00:16:54.650
Was there anything
else that you wanted

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:58.460
to add around that in terms of
why quantum computing is still

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:01.322
far off in the distance for
us for breaking all the codes?

00:17:01.322 --> 00:17:02.780
ADAM LANGLEY: I
mean, I think we've

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:06.319
chatted about how hard it is to
build these quantum computers

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:08.780
and how important it is
to consider error rates.

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:11.589
And don't just think
that an n bit quantum

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:14.450
computer is going to be able
to solve n bit problems.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.420
There's a huge difference
between raw physical qubits

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:20.900
and the theoretical qubits
that we want to build out

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:22.670
many, many raw ones.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:26.270
And the second reason
for don't panic is that--

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:29.972
I said that we have huddled
against this border of what

00:17:29.972 --> 00:17:31.430
we can solve and
how much structure

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:33.800
we put in our problems
to make them efficient.

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:36.590
We can back away from
that border a bit,

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:39.110
and we can still
have cryptography

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:42.640
that is robust in the face
of a quantum adversary.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:45.950
And we don't need to resort
to what is called quantum

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:47.690
cryptography to do that.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:51.980
So quantum cryptography
is putting expensive boxes

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:54.350
of optics on the end
of fiber optic cables

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:58.880
and using quantum phenomenon
to guarantee confidentiality

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:00.430
and so forth.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:02.360
And that's exciting
and interesting,

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:07.160
but it's not what Nick and I
are looking at because we're not

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:10.094
going to have dedicated fiber
optic links between everything

00:18:10.094 --> 00:18:10.760
on the internet.

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:13.270
We'd like to be able to
use the internet we've got.

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:14.420
And we can do that.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:17.720
We can use different problems
with public key cryptography

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.180
that resist quantum
computers, and it's just

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.220
normal software that
runs on normal computers

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.920
and runs over the internet
as we know and love it.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:28.370
MELANIE WARRICK: Great, so
in terms of quantum security,

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:29.600
what are you looking at?

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:32.030
What are the things
that you're assessing

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:35.002
when you think about, long
term, what this looks like?

00:18:35.002 --> 00:18:36.710
NICK SULLIVAN: Right
now, as I mentioned,

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:39.860
a lot of cryptography
is based on these number

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:42.830
theoretic algorithms
like factoring.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:46.370
So RSA-- this is
the standard way

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:47.960
that cryptography has been done.

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:50.930
This was the first algorithm
for public key cryptography

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:52.820
since 1977.

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:58.910
And RSA involves these numbers
that you scramble up and can

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:02.340
encrypt to another person, and
that person can decrypt it.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.670
So being able to
break this requires

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:05.910
you to factor large numbers.

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:08.990
With quantum computers,
it is potentially possible

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:10.520
to do this.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:15.170
So RSA becomes something
that's less safe if there are

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:16.610
large-scale quantum computers.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:19.130
And whether that's going to
happen in the next 10 or 15

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:22.870
years, we don't really know.

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:25.047
There's very small
quantum computers

00:19:25.047 --> 00:19:27.380
that are happening right now,
but they can't necessarily

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:28.460
break something like RSA.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:30.950
So one thing that we're
looking at, as Adam mentioned,

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:33.980
is different
cryptographic algorithms

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:39.200
that are potentially resistant
to the types of things

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:40.670
that quantum computers can do.

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:42.620
So quantum computers,
as we mentioned,

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.299
only have a few things
that they can do better

00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:46.340
than classical computers.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:49.520
In actuality, some
computations are actually

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:53.010
worse than regular
classical computers.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:55.130
So what we're
looking at right now

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:59.480
is finding algorithms that
are resistant to these quantum

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:02.270
computers, or that are
resistant to all known quantum

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:06.770
algorithms, as well as being
resistant to the classical,

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:08.300
traditional computers.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:11.210
As the years progress,
computers still get faster.

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:12.200
Computers get stronger.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.210
People figure out
better algorithms

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:15.320
to solve classical things.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:19.050
So this class of algorithms
to do cryptography

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:21.570
that is resistant
to quantum computers

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:24.090
is called post-quantum
cryptography.

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:27.480
And many folks around the
world are looking at, actually,

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:30.150
how to figure out what the
right post-quantum cryptography

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:30.960
algorithms are.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:33.480
And there are several
different possibilities

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:36.120
and different fields of
research in mathematics

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:41.910
that have promising answers
for what a quantum-resistant

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:44.697
or post-quantum cryptography
algorithm would look like.

00:20:44.697 --> 00:20:46.530
MELANIE WARRICK: And
you told me about NIST,

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:50.350
which I know is running
this large-scale assessment.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:53.970
Is it a competition that they're
doing, the National Institute

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.470
of Standards and Technology?

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:58.720
ADAM LANGLEY: So NIST
referred to it as a process.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:03.150
So NIST is a US
government body which

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.340
have been involved in
standardizing cryptography

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:07.200
for a long time.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:13.830
They standardized PSTAR, and AS,
and a number of other acronyms

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:15.710
that people would have
heard of if they're

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:17.530
paying attention in this space.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:20.190
So they're currently running
what they call a process.

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.080
And they call it a
process to distinguish it

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.990
from a competition, I
think because they expect

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:28.830
to have more than one
"winner," in quotes, i.e,

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:32.920
they will be selecting a
portfolio of possibilities.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.640
And they have a timeline
that stretches out--

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.520
I don't recall precisely--
but some five or six years

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:39.130
from now.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:41.250
And so they invited
groups around the world

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:42.860
to submit proposals.

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:46.180
And at the end of
2017, there were--

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:50.430
I'm not sure-- close to
70 proposals submitted

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:53.580
from different groups for
candidate post-quantum

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:55.210
algorithms.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:57.310
And NIST has published
all of these.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:22:00.150
And so people are now
going through these Round 1

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:03.510
submissions, and
breaking some of them,

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:08.280
and analyzing others,
and whittling them

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.720
down a little bit.

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:11.760
And then at some point,
NIST will publish

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:13.500
the shortlist for Round 2.

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:15.670
And that will continue
for a few years to come.

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:16.920
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:22.200
And in terms of this process, do
you use some of the algorithms

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:26.070
that you see coming from NIST
and experiment with them?

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:30.520
ADAM LANGLEY: Chrome, in 2016,
did do an experiment with one

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:32.430
post-quantum algorithm.

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:35.160
So if you used
Chrome in 2016, you

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:38.490
may well have used a
post-quantum algorithm when

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.600
connecting to Google's service.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:44.370
And we did that both to raise
the profile of this subject

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:50.880
in general and also to check the
viability, because one of the,

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:53.880
it appears, consequences
of post-quantum algorithms

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:56.940
is that they will be less
efficient, either in terms

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:59.790
of speed or in terms of size.

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:04.890
And we had simply never
tried running a key agreement

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:07.410
algorithm that large
over the internet.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.590
And the internet's
very complicated.

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:11.860
You don't always know
what's going to happen.

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:15.510
And so we did this experiment,
looked at latency impacts

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:17.400
and impacts on error rates.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:18.945
And it all went pretty well.

00:23:18.945 --> 00:23:20.340
MARK MANDEL: So you actually
touched on one small thing,

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:21.960
but I'd love to
learn more about.

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:24.210
What are the characteristics
or the differences

00:23:24.210 --> 00:23:26.340
between what's
current cryptography

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:29.370
and what cryptography would look
like in a post-quantum world?

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:30.911
NICK SULLIVAN:
Cryptography right now

00:23:30.911 --> 00:23:33.630
uses several different
primitives that provide

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.670
different security features.

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:38.410
There is symmetric cryptography.

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:41.790
This is cryptography in
which both parties already

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:45.510
have the same key, and
you are making sure

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:47.730
that the data is
confidential so that only

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:51.090
the person with the exact
same key can decrypt it.

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:52.880
This is symmetric encryption.

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:55.830
There's also something
called integrity

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:58.050
that you can add on top of that.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:02.290
Message Authentication Codes,
MACs, are one of those.

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:04.440
And this also requires
symmetric keys.

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.750
So both people have
the exact same key.

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:09.180
So you can send information
across the internet

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:11.190
or across any kind
of untrusted medium.

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:14.560
As long as both parties have the
same key, you can communicate.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:19.710
Now, these keys nowadays
are around 128 bits long.

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:22.680
That's considered to be the
smallest possible key that

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:26.160
will give you a long-term
amount of security.

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:30.630
In a post-quantum quantum world,
because of the square root

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:35.280
level advances in being able
to search through a list,

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:39.150
the only real difference is
that these keys go from 128 bits

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:41.454
to 256 bits.

00:24:41.454 --> 00:24:43.120
So this is something
that we're used to.

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:45.930
And this is something
that we can do.

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:49.890
The bigger differences come
in these so-called asymmetric

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:50.940
algorithms.

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:53.910
The ID is a public
key encryption,

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:55.800
RSA that I mentioned.

00:24:55.800 --> 00:25:01.260
These allow you to, as an
individual, have a private key

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:02.670
as well as a public key.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:05.460
And your public key is
shared with the world,

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:09.300
and anyone can encrypt data
so that only you can decrypt

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.950
it using your public key.

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:12.090
So you take data.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.530
You take the public key.

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:16.080
You scramble up the
data, and only the person

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:19.130
with the associated
private key can decrypt it.

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:21.980
Similarly, there's the
idea of digital signatures,

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:24.770
where you can take
that private key

00:25:24.770 --> 00:25:27.110
and associate it
with a piece of data

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:29.900
so that anyone in the
world with your public key

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:33.710
can then verify that only the
person with the private key

00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:36.690
was able to digitally
sign that data.

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:39.950
So these both provide
ways for folks

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:42.950
to agree on symmetric keys.

00:25:42.950 --> 00:25:46.430
So you go from public key
asymmetric cryptography

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:49.670
to sharing a key and
being able to communicate

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:51.350
across the internet.

00:25:51.350 --> 00:25:53.282
Each one of these
different pieces,

00:25:53.282 --> 00:25:55.490
whether it's symmetric key
cryptography, whether it's

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:58.640
digital signatures, or
public key cryptography,

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:03.320
has a quantum-resistant
post-quantum component to it.

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:07.580
And as Adam mentioned,
these typically

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:09.920
do not have the same
performance characteristics

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:11.460
in terms of computing.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:16.220
So it's either going to cost
a lot more of CPU to compute

00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:19.010
these operations-- so it might
take milliseconds longer than

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:20.560
it would usually--

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:22.850
or the key sizes
are much bigger.

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:27.560
And as I mentioned, symmetric
keys have a 128 bit key size.

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:30.770
They go up to 256.

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:33.800
Typically, right now, for
public key cryptography,

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:37.460
if you're using elliptic
curves, which is very popular,

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:39.840
you have 256 bit numbers.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:43.820
RSA, it's about
2,000 48-bit numbers.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:46.100
When you're talking about
post-quantum cryptography,

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:50.720
these keys can get up into
the 10,000 bits range,

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:54.950
or for some NIST proposals,
the 100,000 bit ranges.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:58.010
And for some joke
NIST proposals,

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:00.020
you can have keys as
large as a terabyte.

00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:01.700
MELANIE WARRICK: How does one
test for quantum computing

00:27:01.700 --> 00:27:04.080
when the quantum computers
are still in development?

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.590
ADAM LANGLEY: So it's
in theory, essentially.

00:27:06.590 --> 00:27:10.160
It is the case that all of
our classical cryptography, we

00:27:10.160 --> 00:27:12.410
don't know that there
aren't efficient

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:14.970
algorithms to break it
on a classical computer.

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:17.620
Our only basis for believing
that there aren't is

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:20.240
that we've looked real hard
and we've never found one.

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:23.010
And so it remains with
quantum computers.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.980
So Peter Shor was
able to come up

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:27.860
with Shor's algorithm
for factoring

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:31.730
long before any quantum computer
existed, because we know,

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:33.740
in theory, the physics.

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:37.860
And so we can theorize
about-- given this problem,

00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:40.070
can we come up with
a quantum algorithm

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:41.730
that efficiently solves it?

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:45.560
And if the answer is
no, then we hypothesize

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:47.690
that it is post-quantum secure.

00:27:47.690 --> 00:27:50.630
But just like
classical cryptography,

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:54.740
we have no proof that there
isn't an efficient algorithm

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.060
out there that nobody's
just found yet.

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:58.970
But that's a situation
we're relatively

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:01.096
comfortable with because
it's always been that way.

00:28:01.096 --> 00:28:02.303
MELANIE WARRICK: Interesting.

00:28:02.303 --> 00:28:04.520
You had listed that you
were interested in diving

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:05.480
into a little bit is--

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:07.880
what is the difference
between confidentiality

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:09.360
versus authenticity?

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.610
NICK SULLIVAN: So this is
also a very important point.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.760
When speaking about the
different types of things

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:16.310
that cryptography
provides, I mentioned

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:18.950
symmetric cryptography
is sending a message

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:22.520
and making sure that only the
person that it's intended for

00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:23.930
can decrypt it.

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:25.170
This is confidentiality.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.650
This is how you keep your
information confidential.

00:28:27.650 --> 00:28:30.920
The other piece is
integrity, which

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:32.930
is, how do you make
sure that someone

00:28:32.930 --> 00:28:34.530
didn't change that message?

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:36.030
Even if they didn't
see what it was,

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:39.520
how are you sure that this
message is in the right order,

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:43.040
that it's exactly the
message that the person sent?

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:45.830
And in a scenario
where somebody is

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:47.890
trying to attack
your cryptography,

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:50.400
these have different timelines.

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:55.190
So if you have the ability to
break an integrity algorithm,

00:28:55.190 --> 00:28:56.990
if someone's already
sent the message,

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:58.270
that doesn't really help you.

00:28:58.270 --> 00:29:00.020
If someone's already
received the message,

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:02.662
it doesn't help you at all
because the message is sent.

00:29:02.662 --> 00:29:03.620
And it's been received.

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:05.190
It's been checked.

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:08.160
So if you have the ability
to break integrity,

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:12.020
you have to be there right now
and intercepting communication,

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:15.830
and changing it, and modifying
it before it gets to the person

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:17.060
that you're talking to.

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:19.020
When it comes to
confidentiality,

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:23.190
you have to be prepared for
attackers in the future.

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:27.290
So if you're communicating
with someone right now,

00:29:27.290 --> 00:29:32.020
and saying something that you
want to be secure for 30 years,

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:34.520
and you're sending it
over an insecure channel,

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:37.070
then you want to make sure
that the confidentiality

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:39.350
algorithm that
you're using is going

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:44.270
to be able to not be
broken for those 30 years.

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:46.790
So sometime in the future,
if someone's collected it,

00:29:46.790 --> 00:29:50.530
they break that algorithm, they
can reveal what it is you said.

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:54.560
And this is actually
the more pressing point

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:56.510
for quantum cryptography.

00:29:56.510 --> 00:30:00.860
If we're talking right now
about digital signatures,

00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:04.970
it's not so important that we
get a digital signature right

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:07.310
now, with respect
to quantum security,

00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:09.950
because in the next five
years or the next 10 years,

00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:12.350
there's not going to be a
large-scale quantum computer.

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:16.410
But in the next 25 years
or the next 30 years,

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:19.180
there's potentially a chance.

00:30:19.180 --> 00:30:23.240
Technology moves rather quickly,
and 25 years is a long time.

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:29.092
So the main focus of what
Chrome's experiment was

00:30:29.092 --> 00:30:30.550
and what we're
looking at right now

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:33.460
for post-quantum
cryptography is, how do we

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:34.970
do this key agreement?

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:37.270
How do we make sure
that the confidentiality

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:40.660
of communication is
actually quantum-resistant?

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:43.960
So having a post-quantum
key agreement algorithm

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:47.081
is very, very important
now because everything

00:30:47.081 --> 00:30:49.330
that you say on the internet,
although encrypted right

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:52.210
now, in the future,
with a quantum computer,

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:55.860
someone should be able
to eventually decrypt it

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:57.025
if they have a copy.

00:30:57.025 --> 00:30:59.441
ADAM LANGLEY: Oh, sorry, I was
just going to add one point

00:30:59.441 --> 00:31:01.960
to that, which is that when
we do these experiments with

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:05.680
post-quantum algorithms-- going
back to how I said that we have

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:09.010
no proof of security for them
or the cryptography we currently

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:09.910
use--

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:13.450
so we combined them
because it's quite possible

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:17.740
that our supposed post-quantum
algorithm might not only

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:19.330
fall to a quantum
computer, it may

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:21.400
fall to a classical computer.

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:25.039
And so when we add
them, we don't replace

00:31:25.039 --> 00:31:26.080
the current cryptography.

00:31:26.080 --> 00:31:28.570
We augment it, and
run both, and then

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:31.630
mix the outputs together
so that the combination is

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:34.420
as strong as the
weakest of the two.

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:38.705
And therefore, at least, by
using a supposedly, quote,

00:31:38.705 --> 00:31:40.330
"quantum algorithm,"
we're at least not

00:31:40.330 --> 00:31:41.537
making things any worse.

00:31:41.537 --> 00:31:43.120
MELANIE WARRICK: Is
there any specific

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:46.030
resources or places
that you'd recommend

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:48.130
if people are interested
in better understanding

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:51.340
post-quantum security,
post-quantum cryptography,

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:52.407
outside of NIST?

00:31:52.407 --> 00:31:54.490
NICK SULLIVAN: I mean,
there are various websites.

00:31:54.490 --> 00:31:57.160
For cutting-edge
research, there's

00:31:57.160 --> 00:32:00.760
an annual conference called
the PQCrypto conference.

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:03.670
And this is where the
latest and greatest

00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:05.330
from the academic
research comes out.

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:09.140
But a lot of this is very,
very new and very ongoing,

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:11.470
and it's rapidly changing.

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:14.200
So every year,
something that was

00:32:14.200 --> 00:32:18.290
considered to be post-quantum
secure last year gets broken.

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:21.250
And this is especially
true within this process.

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:25.750
So there's a number of
websites and resources

00:32:25.750 --> 00:32:28.210
for getting up-to-date with
post-quantum cryptography.

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:34.660
But I think waiting for the end
of the NIST competition/process

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:38.500
to happen is
probably where people

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:41.950
want to get more involved
and more interested in this,

00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:43.750
because otherwise,
you may be overwhelmed

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:45.815
with the rapid rate of change.

00:32:45.815 --> 00:32:47.440
I think this is a
resource that someone

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:50.860
needs to build is an
accessible list of links

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:52.780
about joining the post-quantum.

00:32:52.780 --> 00:32:54.470
I know there's one website--

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:56.830
Adam, on his website.

00:32:56.830 --> 00:33:00.220
ADAM LANGLEY: I wrote an
introduction on lattice-based

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:01.360
once upon a time.

00:33:01.360 --> 00:33:02.770
It's a bit narrow, though.

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:04.311
MELANIE WARRICK:
We'll look for that.

00:33:04.311 --> 00:33:07.510
And speaking of the
Post-QuantumCrypto conference,

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:09.630
my understanding is
that was last week.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.569
Nick, were you at
that conference?

00:33:11.569 --> 00:33:14.110
NICK SULLIVAN: Yeah, I made it
to the Post-Quantum conference

00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:18.790
last week, and we saw a bunch
of interesting proposals that

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:23.020
moved the state-of-the-art even
more past the algorithms that

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:24.820
were submitted to
the NIST contest,

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:27.580
which is kind of
surprising, but kind of not.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:30.490
It was even less
than half a year

00:33:30.490 --> 00:33:34.330
ago that the final proposals
were due for the NIST process,

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:37.010
but several of them have been
broken in interesting ways.

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:40.550
Several of them have been
modified in interesting ways.

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:43.060
And there's even brand
new proposals that

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:45.470
have yet to be cryptoanalyzed.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:49.420
So when you talk about what
the new standards are going

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:51.400
to be for post-quantum
cryptography,

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:54.640
I think what comes through the
first NIST processes are not

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:56.950
going to be the final
ones that people use.

00:33:56.950 --> 00:34:01.690
I think there's just so many
different arenas of research

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:06.490
so active right now that we'll
be discovering new algorithms

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:07.910
for a long time going forward.

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:10.000
And from an implementer's
point of view,

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.280
this is, perhaps,
frustrating, right?

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:15.340
Because you want to pick a
standard, and stick to it,

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:17.560
and make sure that people
have implemented it

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:19.659
correctly and safely.

00:34:19.659 --> 00:34:23.949
I'm looking forward to
more cryptography that's

00:34:23.949 --> 00:34:28.300
oriented towards safe and secure
implementation and deployment

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:31.533
as well as security
against quantum machines.

00:34:31.533 --> 00:34:32.949
MELANIE WARRICK:
Anything specific

00:34:32.949 --> 00:34:35.560
that comes to your mind that
you were most excited to see

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:38.080
or you found most interesting
from that conference?

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:39.496
NICK SULLIVAN: The
main thing that

00:34:39.496 --> 00:34:42.850
resonated with me was
the diversity of types

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:46.550
of mathematics
that were involved.

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:50.320
So a field of cryptography
that I hadn't studied very much

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:52.540
is called code-based
cryptography.

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:56.860
And this uses things like
error-correcting codes

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:59.170
to build cryptosystems.

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:02.260
So rather than using
mathematical properties

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:04.480
like prime numbers
and whatnot, it

00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:07.690
uses these codes that are
used in communication.

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:09.790
And you can build
cryptosystems on this.

00:35:09.790 --> 00:35:12.870
And in fact, one of the first
quantum-resistant algorithms

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:14.500
is based on this type of thing.

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:17.720
So there's cryptography
everywhere.

00:35:17.720 --> 00:35:20.270
You can build
interesting cryptosystems

00:35:20.270 --> 00:35:22.530
from a lot of different arenas.

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:26.700
And it's just fascinating to
see it all evolve and tumble

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:27.200
together.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:30.260
So code-based
cryptography, perhaps,

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:33.980
has some issues where you
have encryption failures once

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:34.530
in a while.

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:37.220
So you have these modes
in which cryptography,

00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:38.870
as you're used to
it, is supposed

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:40.850
to work 100% of the time.

00:35:40.850 --> 00:35:43.770
You lose that in some of
these new constructions.

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:47.900
So I think some of what
is most interesting to me

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:52.040
is cryptographic algorithms
that most closely represent

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:55.310
the intuition that we have
from current cryptography

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:58.580
and that help fit into
our current system

00:35:58.580 --> 00:36:00.740
so that people can actually
deploy them and make

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:02.540
use of them on the internet.

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:03.170
MELANIE WARRICK:
Is there something

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:04.919
you think would be
great if we could bring

00:36:04.919 --> 00:36:07.370
these types of insights or
this type of expertise that

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:11.210
could potentially help expand
post-quantum cryptography

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:12.115
research?

00:36:12.115 --> 00:36:13.490
ADAM LANGLEY: One
of the things I

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:15.470
would like to see
with post-quantum

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:19.220
would be some lessons learned
from earlier cryptography.

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.650
A lot of the
cryptography we use today

00:36:21.650 --> 00:36:24.830
was developed and
standardized in the 1990s.

00:36:24.830 --> 00:36:27.900
And I think there were some
things that, as a world,

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:29.480
we just got wrong there.

00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:34.370
We made things that were
overly complicated, and overly

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:37.970
configurable, and difficult
to implement correctly,

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:41.864
and the consequences of that
have not been good, right?

00:36:41.864 --> 00:36:43.280
The consequences
of those problems

00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:46.500
are bugs and security issues.

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:51.110
And so I think we are now
a lot better at recognizing

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:54.650
these issues and
recognizing, what

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:56.690
is a sensible level
of complexity,

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:59.360
and what are the errors
that implementations

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:00.980
are likely to make?

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:02.630
And then we will
think about, how

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:05.720
do we structure these designs
to avoid these errors?

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:07.340
And so while I
think we're better,

00:37:07.340 --> 00:37:10.490
I would most like to see
post-quantum cryptography

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:14.810
really embracing that
more practical concern.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:18.440
But that has to come down
the road in some cases

00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:22.010
because we are still at the
theoretical point in many

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:23.440
of these algorithms.

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:26.420
They're still in development,
and in some cases, quite

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:27.680
rapid evolution.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:29.540
But if we didn't
repeat the mistakes

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:32.571
of the past in the next
cycle, that'd be very nice.

